linker-dht 14.2.4 remain owed.\ndocs/design/yin.vm.ucf-revisions.md:392:    C12 sentences in 7.7.7 (enrollment retry), 7.11.1 (the stage-C\ndocs/design/yin.vm.ucf-revisions.md:417:    tail, documented in UCF 7.11. The doc amendments these stages\ndocs/design/yin.vm.ucf-revisions.md:493:- UCF 7.11's warning stands until each blocker closes with the tests\ndocs/design/yin.vm.ucf-revisions.md:503:    format.md S7.11's contract-revision-publication blocker. Prior\ndocs/design/yin.vm.ucf-revisions.md:507:direct citation.\" Against it, UCF 7.11 (in-tree today) still\ndocs/design/yin.vm.ucf-revisions.md:514:    whose complete revision history is not yet published, as S7.11\ndocs/design/yin.vm.ucf-revisions.md:518:Ruling: the sign-off and UCF 7.11 stood at the time. semantic.md 2.4\ndocs/design/yin.vm.ucf-revisions.md:622:UCF 7.11's safepoint harness must run each 7.4.1 row, including both\ndocs/design/yin.vm.ucf-revisions.md:689:7.4.1-7.4.3, 7.11); the deleted v3 `yin.vm.semantic.md` (header, 2.2,\ndocs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:10:> S7.11 lists the acceptance blockers that keep this warning standing until\ndocs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:36:(S7.6.1); S7.11 records the remaining definition-frame obligations.\ndocs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:41:(7.2.1, 7.4.3, 7.7.8, 7.9, 7.11.1). The amendment is published design:\ndocs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:66:and 7.6.1a (the source association and its completeness rule); 7.11 and\ndocs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:67:7.11.1 require the frame validator and cross-engine canonical equality\ndocs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:86:Every sentence in §7.2–§7.9 is a rule of the proposed protocol. §7.11 records\ndocs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:556:transition; see the revision log in S7.11. The de Bruijn images carry\ndocs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:1331:  publication is an acceptance blocker (S7.11).\ndocs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:2062:retention machine and the unavailable-lane semantics are in 7.11.\ndocs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:2826:independently (7.11).\ndocs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:2848:## §7.11 Acceptance blockers for the implementation phase\ndocs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:3027:### 7.11.1 Blocker-closure acceptance matrix\ndocs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.v2-amendment.md:9:(UCF), especially 7.2.1, 7.3, 7.4, 7.5, 7.6, 7.7.4 and 7.11.1.\ndocs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md:2178:11. **The primitive-profile registry** — inherited from UCF §7.11. Every\ndocs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md:2200:    `yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md` §7.11's contract-revision-\n### 1.3 Fork custody slot and the retained-FFI seam\n\n**Fork custody slot nil (a, owner 2026-10-14).**\n- Code: `rows.cljc:1763-1769` a nil header leaves the root's custody slot nil; `:1701-1713` `header-custody` writes the five-key slot only from a header; `custody-baseline` (`:1286`) refuses operation ids on a fork tree; `holder/driver.cljc:1026` `fork-step`; `holder/export.cljc:168-179` `check-header!`, `:387-399` `header-agrees?` (nil header ⇔ fork baseline).\n- Tests: `ucf/rows_custody_test.cljc:35` (slot by kind), `:58` (what custody forbids), `:248` `a-fork-is-refused-under-exclusive`.\n- Design: continuation §2.5-2.6 (amended after 7), UCF §7.2.1 :305-313 (\"version 0 is fork only\").\n- What would trigger a change: a composition that wants a fork root later promoted to exclusive custody, or fork provenance (an origin chain on forks). Effort then: slot grammar, baseline, three tests, about one day. Risk of leaving: none; two resumers of a fork value diverge into distinct incarnations as UCF §7.10 :2835 states.\n\n**Retained-FFI seam not built (a, owner 2026-10-13, 6c Q2 \"open\" but ruled not to build).**\n- Code: both backends' `:request-sent` re-park build the response wait over the composition's own call pair: `debruijn/register.cljc:511`, `debruijn/stack.cljc:567` (`ffi/response-wait-entry entry (:call-id entry)`); the semantic kernel honours a carried pair (`semantic.cljc:105-109`). The harness keeps row 4 out of law 5 by `unlowerable #{[4 :stack] [4 :register]}` (`safepoint_harness_test.cljc:1711-1719`).\n- Design: continuation §4.3 :917-948, §4.6 limit 5, §11 6c Q2.\n- **Risk of leaving it, and the one real finding here.** Continuation §4.6 (:1097-1109) says plainly that rows 4, 7-9 and 17 \"are excluded by the harness only, not by a general pre-attach fragment gate\". `layout/lower-defect` refuses `:ambiguous-image`, `:installs`, `:free-env-value` and `:continuation-registers`, not a retained FFI pending. A real composition that lowers a row-4-shaped task into a backend receiver attaches it and it never wakes (codex probe 3 reproduced the hang on all four such pairs). UCF §7.11 :2923-2924 requires backends \"to refuse the unsupported remainder before attach\". The owner's \"refuse-the-remainder requirement is not evidence of a general gate\" (continuation :1109) records the discrepancy but leaves the hazard.\n- What a future need would trigger: the four-line seam itself (half a day, raises law 5 to 366/96) if any composition migrates retained-FFI tasks into backends. Independent of that: a pre-attach refusal `:yin.k/unsupported :retained-ffi` in `lower-defect` (half a day, one refusal test per backend) is compliant with \"not widened\" and closes the silent-hang hazard. Recommended (§6 item 2).\n\n### 1.4 Section 10 items 1-8, spot-checked in the tree\n**Amended after 6c (2026-10-13), the seam has landed; what the code\ndoes for a tail.** 6c-pre merged (`296f75fd`,\n`stack_tail_completion_test.cljc`); the paragraph above is now a reading\nof code. `lower-state` for a canonical `tail` into the stack kernel emits\n`{:pc (+ off (a->h site) 1) :resume-mode :return-result :stack (the K\nslices, in order) :frames (get fill :frames []) :continuation …}`, with\n`:store-of` only when `fill` names one; into the register kernel `{:site-pc\n(+ off site) :pc (+ off site 1) :regs [] :live [] :dest nil :resume-mode\n:return-result :frames (get fill :frames [])}` likewise. The lift of a\nstack entry decides `tail?` by `(code/tail-call? (inst seg site))` at the\nA site, the register lift by the entry's `:resume-mode`;\n`layout-test/tail-lowering-is-unobservable` lowers rows 6 and 20 into\nboth kernels with `[]`/nil and with junk `:frames`/`:store-of` and sees\nequal observables (§5.6 item 8, four cases). *Why:* the seam was a\nspecification when this section was written; it is now code, and the\nlayout's tail shapes are stated as emitted.\n\n**Amended after 6c (2026-10-13), the retained-FFI response-pair seam\n(codex D2, accepted by R6 as a seam specification; the last sentence is\nR2's). Amended 2026-10-13 (backends): the seam is REJECTED by the owner\n(§11, 6c Q2); the specification below is kept as the record of what was\nproposed and is not to be built.** On completion of a retained FFI request, each de Bruijn kernel\npreserves the carried response cursor and response stream when\nconstructing the response wait. If both are present, response-wait-entry\nis overridden with cursor-ref {:type :cursor-ref :id response-cursor}\nand stream-id response-stream. A partial carried pair is rejected; the\nkernel's composition-owned response pair is used only for entries that\ncarry neither field. Response decoding precedes delivery, including tail\ndelivery. Row 4 must continue on all six ordered engine pairs with the\nreceiving composition's call pair distinct from the source's pair. This\nseam was slice 6c-post-D2, a kernel edit of its own, gated by the owner;\nit was not a prerequisite of 6c. **Amended 2026-10-13 (backends):** the\nowner rejected it. Retained FFI across a de Bruijn receiver is outside\nthe backends' documented fragment (§4.6); `unlowerable` stays `#{[4\n:stack] [4 :register]}` and the law-5 counts stay 350 and 92 (was \"when\nit lands, `unlowerable` is emptied and the counts rise to 366 and 96\").\n*What the code does:*\nboth kernels' `:request-sent` re-park builds `ffi/response-wait-entry`\nover the composition's own call pair (`register.cljc:508-514`,\n`stack.cljc:564-570`), so a carried row 4 never wakes on a de Bruijn\nreceiver (codex probe 3 reproduced it on all four such pairs); the\nharness's `unlowerable` set `#{[4 :stack] [4 :register]}` keeps those\ntransfers out of law 5 (§5.3). The semantic kernel's\n`register-restore` already honours the carried pair\n(`semantic_register.cljc:105-111`). *Why:* phase 6 edits no kernel but\n6c-pre's one seam (§8.5).\n**Amended after 7 (2026-10-14)** — Reason: the owner retains the backends without widening their fragment.\nThis second seam remains outside the documented fragment (§4.6), with\n6c Q2 left open; retaining the kernels does not implement it.\n\n### 4.4 Positional lexical frames, the free environment and `E`\n\n**DECIDED** by frozen §2.1 and §2.4 (`E_c[ps ↦ a]` by `bind-params`),\nphase-5 §1.2 (the owner chain), and the kernels' resolution of free\nnames through the task-wide `free-env` (`stack.cljc:683`:\n`(engine/resolve-var free-env …)`). Both de Bruijn kernels hold the\n5. **Retained FFI across a de Bruijn receiver** (§4.3, 6c Q2\n   rejected; matrix row 4): the backend's re-park names its own call\n   pair. Row 4 lifts from a de Bruijn source and is not lowered into a\n   de Bruijn receiver (`unlowerable`).\n\nEach limit is pinned by a refusal test (§8.3 items 12, 14, 15; §5.3)\nand is a limit of the backends, never of the carrier or of the\ncanonical kernel. The owner-accepted native-lane matrix (61 law-4 cases\nover three lanes, 350 law-5 transfers; 16 and 92 in the quick tier) is\nthe supported fragment measured, and its justification is this\nsection, not any shelf life (§11, 6c Q1).\n\n**Cross-references.** Frozen design §4.3 and §10 phases 5, 6 and 8;\nderivations design §8.2-8.3 (the lowerings and what phase 8 deletes);\nUCF §7.4.2 (layouts); `yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md` §10 item 6 (the\nthree repository lanes); §0.1 ruling 8, §5.3, §8.5, §9 and §11 here.\n\n---\n\n### 4.6 The retained backend fragment\n\n**Amended after 7 (2026-10-14; names updated at the 2026-10-15 cutover)** — Reason: the owner retains both de Bruijn kernels as backends of the canonical semantic VM.\nThe stack and register kernels are the canonical kernel's two backends,\nchecked through the layouts on the shared fragment in §5.3. They remain\nthrough and after phase 8; the fragment is not widened. It excludes link\nand install rows 7-12 and 17, and row 4 is not lowered into either\nbackend while the retained-FFI seam remains absent.\n**Amended after 7 (2026-10-14)** — Reason: the code's four pre-attach checks do not gate the whole harness fragment.\n`lower-defect` checks, in order, image collision (`:ambiguous-image`),\ninstall children (`:installs`), closures or continuations in the free\nenvironment (`:free-env-value`, including nested collections), and, for\nthe register backend, primitive functions in wait or parked-state\npayloads (`:continuation-registers`). These refuse before attachment\non lower (§4.1-4.4 and §7.2). Of the excluded rows, only the install\nrows 10-12 are refused before attach (`lower-defect` `:installs`); rows\n4, 7-9 and 17 are excluded by the harness only, not by a general\npre-attach fragment gate (§11 6c Q2). The harness's `link-rows` excludes\nrows 7-12 and 17 from backend lanes; `unlowerable` excludes row 4 when\nthe receiver is a stack or register backend. The owner's\nrefuse-the-remainder requirement is not evidence of a general gate.\nStage E uses the canonical semantic kernel only. A is the only code identity;\nnative images are derived forms, and the source AST remains retrievable\nthrough the linker's source-index, fetch and admission path (§6.1).\n\n## 5. The safepoint harness\n\n---\ndescription: Build, lint, test commands, TDD guidelines, and bracket debugging for Clojure, ClojureScript, Node.js, and ClojureDart\n---\n\n# BUILD & TEST\n\n## Commands\n\n```sh\n# Tests (Babashka runner)\nbb test              # All three lanes (JVM, Node, Dart), fast default form: no slow tests\nbb test:all          # Every test on every lane, slow ones included (JVM takes 20+ min)\nbb test:slow         # Only the slow tests, on every lane\n# One lane alone: <task>:clj, <task>:cljs, <task>:cljd\nbb test:clj          # JVM, fast: skips ^:slow (bare `clj -M:test` runs them too)\nbb test:cljs         # Node (shadow-cljs :cljs alias), fast: guarded slow bodies print SKIP\nbb test:cljd         # Dart (needs flutter on PATH), fast: guarded slow bodies print SKIP\nbb test:all:clj      # JVM, no filter: `clojure -M:test`\nbb test:all:cljs     # Node with DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\nbb test:all:cljd     # Dart with DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\nbb test:slow:clj     # JVM: `-i :slow` (long-loops-test, ~14 min)\nbb test:slow:cljs    # Node: only the namespaces that use dao.test-slow/guard, DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\nbb test:slow:cljd    # Dart: only those namespaces' generated tests, DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\n# Slow tests: tag a deftest ^:slow. The JVM runner then excludes it from `bb test:clj`.\n# Node and Dart have no tag filter, so a .cljc slow test also wraps its body in\n# (dao.test-slow/guard \"name\" (fn [] ...)); it prints SKIP unless DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1.\n# Node and Dart select slow tests by NAMESPACE (derived by grepping `slow/guard`\n# under test/), so the non-slow tests inside those namespaces also run there.\n# On Node, shadow also runs the test namespaces those nine require (about\n# seven more, e.g. lower-portable-test); on Dart only the nine namespaces run.\n# Run test:slow / test:all before a big merge or a commit that touches many parts.\n# Run one Dart lane at a time per machine (the limit is cores and the Flutter cache,\n# not subsystems). On the many-core remote hosts see \"Remote test servers\" below for\n# which lanes may overlap.\n#\n# When to tag a test slow: when it takes more than about 5 s on any lane (the\n# Python e2e tests sit on a ~3 s floor, so their JVM cut is 3.0 s). The\n# default lanes must stay in minutes; 22 JVM tests (0.75% of the suite) were\n# 81% of its test time. Tag it ^:slow on the JVM; if it is a .cljc test, also\n# wrap its body in dao.test-slow/guard so Node and Dart skip it by default. A\n# test that is slow only on Dart (dao.jing.dht-test/unproven-chunks-... takes\n# 72 s there, 3.6 s on the JVM) is a candidate for a speed investigation first.\n# Python slow set (2026-10-06): 65 ^:slow and 15 guard-only tests in the\n# yang.python.antlr e2e, e2e-c1, prelude-parity, safepoint, float-address\n# and int-contract tests; a Python slice landing runs `bb test:sub:clj yang.python --slow`\n# (or `clojure -M:test -i :slow -n <ns>`).\n# While iterating, use the Gate Ladder (see below): G0 iterate via `bb test:sub:clj <own>`\n# or `clojure -M:test -n <ns>`. Do not run the full `bb test` while iterating.\n# At slice checkpoints, run G1 (`bb test:sub <own>`). Before landing a slice, run\n# G2 (`bb test:changed` in the worktree across all three lanes).\n# Full repo-wide `bb test` (G3) is the mandatory landing gate on master after merge.\n# Cross-host bugs show only on Node or Dart, so tri-host verification is mandatory\n# for every gate from G1 upward. Run one lane set at a time per machine to avoid\n# contention (a laptop has 8 cores; the 24- and 48-core remote hosts have room to\n# overlap lanes, see \"Remote test servers\" below).\n# Measured fast-lane times (2026-10-03): bb test about 14 min = JVM 5.5 + Node\n# 3.8 + Dart 4.3, plus builds. test:slow: Node and Dart about 5 min each (mostly\n# compile); the JVM half is estimated at 17 min (long-loops-test alone is 14),\n# not yet run end to end. test:all: JVM 20+ min, also an estimate.\nnpm test             # Node.js tests\n\n# Cross-host peers the JVM lane spawns. `bb test` builds both. A bare\n# `bb test:clj` builds only the Node REPL (it depends on build:yin-repl-node);\n# without build:yin-repl-peer first, the Dart-peer tests skip with a printed notice.\n# Under `bb test:sub` and `bb test:changed`, prerequisites are derived automatically.\nbb build:yin-repl-peer   # Dart exe build/yin-repl-peer (yin.repl R5 pairs)\nbb build:yin-repl-node   # Node REPL target/yin-repl.js; REQUIRED by\n                         # yin.repl.dht-process-test (JVM-to-Node DHT reader,\n                         # and the linker L5 Node reader that requires a module\n                         # by name): `clojure -M:test` without it fails that test\n```\n\n### Changed-only tests: `bb test:changed`\n\n`bb test:changed` (src/dev/affected.clj) runs the fast tests a change can\nreach: the reverse require closure of the files changed since the\nmerge-base with master, plus fixtures that mention a changed resource path,\ntests that walk a changed tree, and each src file's `_test` by convention.\nA walker is a file calling file-seq, fs/glob, fs/list-dir,\nfs/walk-file-tree or .listFiles; each string literal argument of its\nio/file, fs/file, fs/path, fs/glob, java.io.File. or File. calls is a root\n(let-bound roots count), and a change under a root selects it. The three\nlanes run in parallel (logs: target/profile/changed-<lane>.log) after the\nneeded builds. `test:changed:list` shows what and why; `:clj`/`:cljs`/\n`:cljd` run one lane; `--base REF`; `--changed FILE...` (absolute OK; a\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
